{"assets":[],"contestId":"670","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/670/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"670A - Holidays"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"There are many ways to solve this problem. Let's talk about one of them. At first we need to write a function, which takes the start day of the year and calculate the number of days off in such year. To make it let's iterate on the days of the year and will check every day — is it day off or no. It is easy to show that if the first day of the year equals to the first day of the week (i.e. this day is Monday) in this year will be minimum possible number of the days off. If the first day of the year equals to the first day off of the week (i.e. this day is Saturday) in this year will be maximum possible number of the days off. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/670/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"670B - Game of Robots"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"To solve this problem we need to brute how many identifiers will called robots in the order from left to right. Let's solve this problem in one indexing. Let the current robot will call "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" identifiers. If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" > 0 let's make "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and go to the next robot. Else we need to print "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"], where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the array with robots identifiers and end our algorithm."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/670/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"670C - Cinema"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We need to use "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"map"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-ом (let's call it "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cnt"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") and calculate how many scientists knows every language (i. e. "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cnt"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] equals to the number of scientists who know the language number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"). Let's use the pair "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"res"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", where we will store the number of \\textit{very pleased} scientists and the number of \\textit{almost satisfied} scientists. At first let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"res"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"make"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"_"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"pair"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(0, 0). Now we need to iterate through all movies beginning from the first. Let the current movie has the number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"res"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","tex